# Assert Invariants (C/C)

Distinguish **programming errors** (assert) from **expected runtime outcomes** (handle explicitly).

## Use `assert()` for

- Required pointer param is NULL (`!task`, `!aset`, `!aset->ids`)
- Enum/index out of range when caller must pass a valid value
- Internal state that should already be set up (`task->c`, `bc`, `core`)
- Buffer/capacity already full when caller must not overflow

## Handle explicitly (not assert) for

- Allocation failed (`calloc` returned NULL) — propagate error (`return NULL` / cleanup)
- Lookup/cache miss (data may legitimately not exist) — return false, skip, log
- Optional parameter that may be NULL by design — branch on NULL
- `free()` / destroy accepting NULL — null-safe early return is fine

## Do not combine checks

Do not mix programming-error checks with runtime checks in one silent-return block. Split them:

```c
// BAD — hides caller bugs
if( !task || !aset || !aset->ids )
    return;

// GOOD
assert(task && aset && aset->ids);
if( aset->count <= 0 )
{
    dat2_anim_archive_set_init(&task->aset, 0);
    return;
}
```

## Style

- Use `<assert.h>`
- Prefer separate `assert(...)` lines over long compound conditions
- Add a short string message when the condition is not self-evident: `assert(entry && "Model must be inserted into hmap")`
- Do not add defensive silent returns for "impossible" internal state

## When editing existing code

If you touch a function with silent-return guards on required pointers or internal invariants, convert them to asserts in the same change (minimal scope — only the function being edited).
